  "ageMin": 10,
  "ageMax": 10,
  "minutes": 8,
  "players": "6 players: A1 (first carrier), A2 (first attacking support), A3 (next carrier); D1 (first tackler), D2 (passive shield holder), D3 (second defender). One supervising coach; rotate all six roles after each repetition.",
  "equipment": "1 size 4 rugby ball, 1 suitable contact shield, 7 marker cones as shown. Optional spare ball and extra boundary cones. Use a clear 20 m × 15 m practice area with runoff.",
  "instructions": "Set two queues facing each other as shown, with blue attacking towards the bottom of the original diagram. Keep the seven cones in their shown arrangement. A1 reads the space and chooses a route around the central cone line. Pause to show both possibilities; the example then demonstrates a separate controlled tackle by D1. D1 releases and clears the area; A1 presents the ball towards blue support. D2 approaches first with the passive shield; A2 arrives from the attacking side and rehearses a controlled clearout. A3 stays available, collects the exposed ball when it is playable, moves left and meets D3. Stop after the second tackle and practise release/presentation before resetting. Suggested 8 min: 2 min walkthrough, 4 min coached repetitions, 2 min review/rotation. Animation seconds are illustrative, not contact targets.",
  "coaching": "A1: carry securely in two hands, scan the defender, remain balanced, keep head out of the opponent’s headspace and present promptly towards support. D1/D3: track, lower under control, wrap arms and release immediately; use a tackle height below the base of the sternum. A2: arrive from own side, eyes up, stable feet, head and shoulders above hips; in live play bind before or as contact occurs. D2: hold the shield passively and follow the coach’s instructions; no collision contest. A3: stay onside, communicate, wait for a playable ball, collect with two hands and accelerate into space only when clear. Reward ball availability, safe posture, timing and decision-making rather than force.",
  "regression": "Walk through with D1/D3 giving a two-hand touch; A1 lowers to the ground independently under coach instruction. D2 marks the defender position without contact; A2 demonstrates approach and stance, then A3 collects an exposed stationary ball. Increase space or pause each phase.",
  "progression": "Once safe technique is established, vary the side of the first tackle and the timing of the passive defender. Ask A2 to decide whether support is needed and A3 to choose an available channel. Use controlled age-appropriate opposition only with qualified supervision; keep the same U10 participation limits. Rotate attacker/defender roles between repetitions.",
  "questions": "Where should A1 put the ball? What must D1 do immediately after the tackle? Which side must A2 and D2 approach from? When is A3 allowed to pick up the ball? Where is the space for the next carry?",
  "notes": "This is a six-player practice, not a match formation. D2 arrives first by design, not because defenders are required to arrive first. The shield models opposition; shield contact is not a literal live ruck bind. The first ruck marker is removed in the final diagram because the ruck ends when the ball leaves; A1, A2 and D2 recover safely. The studio’s default ATTACK arrow points up; your blue line actually attacks down initially, then A3 moves left. The setup has been re-centred, gates widened and queue spacing opened. The approach and first tackle now have separate steps. One visual decision shows the two routes around the central cones; it does not branch the animation. Confirm current RFU Appendix 4 and club policy before delivery; the 2026–27 referee summary was accessible but the direct official appendix returned 403.",
